use std
into look.amni

Imports: os, math, gfx, gfx.linalg and gfx.gltf. This file is a module with no main. It gives the avatar's materials their runtime shading models after loading and before the avatar is added to the scene: skin, eye and cloth shading in the Helium surface shader (`gltf::Material` fields `shading`, `shade_a`, `shade_b`, `shade_c` and `detail`), tiling detail maps it generates itself, and per-vertex curvature and thickness for skin. Every module-level name starts with `lk_` because the view imports several modules whose globals are unqualified. Vector math uses `linalg::v3`, `linalg::dot3`, `linalg::cross3`, `linalg::length3` and `linalg::unit3`, always qualified. Every real-valued helper here takes and returns f32; convert with `as real` only around `sqrt`, `sin`, `exp` and `floor` calls.

Module state: `var lk_tex int[>..]` (texture index of each generated detail tile, by kind).

`lk_hash(x int, y int, s int) f32` is a deterministic hash of three integers into [0, 1): start with `h = (x * 73856093) ^ (y * 19349663) ^ (s * 83492791)` in int, then three rounds of `h = (h ^ (h >> 13)) * 1274126177` keeping the low 31 bits with `& 2147483647`, and return `(h & 16777215) as f32 / 16777216.0`.

`lk_noise(u f32, v f32, p int, s int) f32` is smooth periodic value noise on the unit square with p cells per side: with `x = u * p`, `y = v * p`, `i = floor(x)`, `j = floor(y)` (ints), fractions `fx = x - i`, `fy = y - j` smoothed by `t * t * (3 - 2 t)`, the four corner values are `lk_hash(((i + a) % p + p) % p, ((j + b) % p + p) % p, s)` for a, b in 0 and 1, blended bilinearly with the smoothed fractions.

`lk_tube(d f32, r f32) f32` is the height profile of a round yarn at distance d from its center line: `sqrt(max(0, 1 - (d / r)^2))`.

`lk_jersey(fx f32, fy f32) f32` is one jersey-knit stitch in its cell (fx, fy in [0, 1)): two legs of a V whose center lines are at x = 0.5 - 0.3 fy and x = 0.5 + 0.3 fy; return `max(lk_tube(abs(fx - (0.5 - 0.3 * fy)), 0.21), lk_tube(abs(fx - (0.5 + 0.3 * fy)), 0.21)) * (0.72 + 0.28 * sin(pi * fy))`.

`lk_weave(u f32, v f32, n int, over int, s int) f32` is a twill weave with n threads per side of the tile and a repeat of 4: cell `i = floor(u * n)`, `j = floor(v * n)`, local `fx = u * n - i`, `fy = v * n - j`; the warp thread i floats over the weft at cell (i, j) when `((i - j) % 4 + 4) % 4 < over`. Each warp thread has a thickness `tw = 0.85 + 0.3 * lk_hash(i, 7, s)` and each weft thread `tf = 0.9 + 0.2 * lk_hash(j, 11, s)`. With `r = ((i - j) % 4 + 4) % 4`, a float spans several cells, so the position along it is `ta = (over - 1 - r + fy) / over` on a warp float and `tb = (r - over + fx) / (4 - over)` on a weft float. On a warp float the height is `0.15 + 0.85 * tw * lk_tube(abs(fx - 0.5), 0.5) * (0.7 + 0.3 * sin(pi * ta))`, on a weft float `0.15 + 0.85 * tf * lk_tube(abs(fy - 0.5), 0.5) * (0.7 + 0.3 * sin(pi * tb))`; clamp to [0, 1].

`lk_height(kind int, u f32, v f32) f32` is the tile height in [0, 1] at (u, v), periodic on the unit square, with the fiber noise `fz = lk_noise(u, v, 97, kind + 3)`:
kind 0, skin pores: on a 23 × 23 cell grid, for each of the 9 cells (ci + a, cj + b) around the cell (ci, cj) holding the point (a and b from -1 to 1, cell indices wrapped modulo 23 for hashing but not for the distance), the cell's pore sits at `(c + 0.15 + 0.7 * lk_hash(cx, cy, 1)) / 23` in each axis (c the unwrapped cell index, cx, cy the wrapped ones; the y offset uses seed 2), exists when `lk_hash(cx, cy, 3) < 0.8`, has radius `(0.18 + 0.2 * lk_hash(cx, cy, 4)) / 23` and depth `0.5 + 0.5 * lk_hash(cx, cy, 5)`; the pit is `depth * (1 - smoothstep)^2` where smoothstep runs from 0 at distance 0 to 1 at the radius (use `t * t * (3 - 2 t)` of the clamped ratio, so the pit is 0 beyond the radius); take the largest pit p and return `clamp(0.8 - 0.7 * p + 0.12 * (lk_noise(u, v, 47, 9) - 0.5) + 0.05 * (fz - 0.5), 0, 1)`;
kind 1, jersey knit: 9 wales by 11 courses, `lk_jersey(u * 9 - floor(u * 9), v * 11 - floor(v * 11)) * 0.9 + 0.1 * fz`;
kind 2, 1×1 rib knit: 11 wales by 13 courses; with column `c = floor(u * 11)`, `fx = u * 11 - c`, `fy = v * 13 - floor(v * 13)`: an even column gives `0.3 + 0.7 * lk_jersey(fx, fy)`, an odd one `0.12 + 0.12 * lk_tube(abs(fx - 0.5), 0.5)`; then times 0.92 plus 0.08 fz;
kind 3, 2/2 wool twill: `lk_weave(u, v, 8, 2, 3) * 0.85 + 0.15 * fz`;
kind 4, 3/1 denim twill: `lk_weave(u, v, 8, 3, 4) * 0.88 + 0.12 * fz`;
kind 5, brushed fleece: `0.5 + 0.28 * (lk_noise(u, v, 7, 5) - 0.5) + 0.2 * (lk_noise(u, v, 17, 6) - 0.5) + 0.32 * (fz - 0.5)`, clamped to [0, 1];
kind 7, fine knit for the briefs: 13 wales by 17 courses of `lk_jersey`, times 0.9 plus 0.1 fz;
any other kind (6), lace net: two families of diagonal threads 8 per tile, `e1 = abs((u + v) * 8 - floor((u + v) * 8 + 0.5))`, `e2 = abs((u - v) * 8 - floor((u - v) * 8 + 0.5))`, result `max(lk_tube(e1, 0.13), lk_tube(e2, 0.13)) * 0.9 + 0.1 * fz`.

`lk_make_tile(kind int, n int, bump f32) int` renders an n × n RGBA tile of kind into the glTF pools and returns its texture index. For each pixel (x, y) (y downward), with `u = (x + 0.5) / n` and `v = (y + 0.5) / n` and `e = 1.0 / n`: `h = lk_height(kind, u, v)`, `dx = lk_height(kind, u + e, v) - lk_height(kind, u - e, v)`, `dy = lk_height(kind, u, v + e) - lk_height(kind, u, v - e)` (heights wrap because the functions are periodic; wrap u and v into [0, 1) by subtracting their floor first); the slope vector `s = linalg::unit3(linalg::v3(-dx * bump, dy * bump, 1.0))`; red `= s.x * 0.5 + 0.5`, green `= s.y * 0.5 + 0.5`, blue (a roughness modulation where 0.5 means unchanged) `= 0.5 + 0.18 * (1 - h) + 0.07 * (lk_noise(u, v, 31, kind + 11) - 0.5)` for kind 0, else `0.5 + 0.12 * (0.5 - h) + 0.1 * (lk_noise(u, v, 41, kind + 11) - 0.5)`, alpha (cavity) `= 0.3 + 0.7 * h` for kind 0, else `0.5 + 0.5 * h`; each channel clamped to [0, 1] and stored as `(c * 255 + 0.5) as int as byte`. Append the bytes to `gltf::pixels` (remember `first = gltf::pixels.len` before), push `gltf::Image { width: n, height: n, pixels: gltf::Span { first, n * n * 4 }, ok: true, .. }` to `gltf::images`, push `gltf::Texture { gltf::images.len - 1, false, false, true, 10497, 10497 }` to `gltf::textures` and return `gltf::textures.len - 1`.

`lk_scale(asset int, mat int, tile f32) float4` is the detail texture transform for material index `mat` (absolute in `gltf::materials`) so that one tile covers `tile` meters along each texture axis: over every primitive of every mesh of the asset whose `material == mat`, and every triangle (positions at floats 0..2 and uv0 at floats 6..7 of `gltf::verts[(prim.vfirst + local index) * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS ..]`, local indices from `gltf::indices[prim.ifirst ..]`, `prim.icount` of them), with edges `e1 = p1 - p0`, `e2 = p2 - p0`, uv deltas `(du1, dv1)`, `(du2, dv2)` and `det = du1 * dv2 - du2 * dv1` (skip the triangle when `abs(det) < 1e-12`), the surface derivatives are `T = (e1 * dv2 - e2 * dv1) / det` (meters per unit u) and `B = (e2 * du1 - e1 * du2) / det` (meters per unit v); with the triangle area `a = 0.5 * length3(cross3(e1, e2))`, add `a * length3(T)` to `su`, `a * length3(B)` to `sv` and a to `aw`. When aw is 0 return `float4 { 1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 }`; else return `float4 { su / aw / tile, 0.0, 0.0, sv / aw / tile }`.

Grid state: `var lk_gt int[>..]` (global vertex indices, three per occluder triangle), `var lk_gs int[>..]` (cell start offsets, cells + 1), `var lk_gl int[>..]` (triangle indices per cell), `var lk_glo = float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }`, `var lk_gx = 0`, `var lk_gy = 0`, `var lk_gz = 0`.

`lk_grid(asset int, lo float3, hi float3)` builds a uniform grid of 0.02 m cells over the box lo..hi (cell counts per axis `floor(extent / 0.02) + 1`, origin `lk_glo = lo`) holding every triangle of every mesh of the asset except meshes named "hair", "braid_hair", "beard", "lashes", "eye_occ" and "scalp" (cards and alpha shells do not block light), skipping triangles whose bounding box misses the box; each triangle is listed in every cell its bounding box overlaps (cell coordinates clamped to the grid; count, prefix sums, fill).

`lk_cast(o float3, d float3, len f32) f32` returns the distance to the nearest grid triangle along the unit ray from o in direction d, or `len` when none is closer: walk only the cells the segment from o to `o + d * len` passes through with the Amanatides-Woo 3D DDA (start in the clamped cell of o; per axis the step is the sign of d, tMax the ray distance to the next cell boundary and tDelta = 0.02 / abs(d), infinite when d is 0 on that axis; stop when the next boundary is beyond len, the walk leaves the grid, or the current cell starts beyond the best hit so far), and in each visited cell test its listed triangles with the Möller-Trumbore test (determinant magnitude below 1e-12 misses), keeping the smallest hit distance t with `0.0002 < t < len`.

`lk_skin_geo(asset int)` writes curvature (1/m) and thickness (m) into uv1 (floats 8 and 9) of every vertex of the asset's meshes named "skin" or "face". Gather their primitives' global vertices and triangles (global vertex index = `prim.vfirst + local index`). Curvature: for each triangle edge (a, b) in both directions, add `abs(dot3(na - nb, pa - pb)) / max(dot3(pa - pb, pa - pb), 1e-12)` to vertex a's sum and 1 to its count (positions floats 0..2, normals 3..5); the vertex curvature is sum / max(count, 1), clamped to at most 250. Vertices split at seams or hard edges share a position, so then give every group of vertices with the same position (positions quantized to 0.1 mm into an integer key, grouped with a sort of vertex indices by key) the mean curvature of the group, and finally smooth once: each vertex takes the mean of its own curvature and the mean curvature of its edge neighbours (from the triangle edges, using the values from before this pass). Thickness: call `lk_grid(asset, lo, hi)` with the bounding box of the skin and face vertices grown by 0.06 m on every side; for each vertex with unit normal n, the raw thickness is `t = lk_cast(p - n * 0.0004, -n, 0.03)`. A thin part only lets light through when its back side is open, so for every vertex with t < 0.015 measure the openness of the back side at `b = p - n * (t + 0.0008)`: with `d = -n` and two unit vectors u, w perpendicular to it (u from the cross product of d with (0, 1, 0), or with (1, 0, 0) when d is nearly vertical), cast `lk_cast(b, dir, 0.04)` along d itself (weight 1) and along `unit3(d * cos(a) + (u * cos(phi) + w * sin(phi)) * sin(a))` for a in 0.45 and 0.9 radians and phi = 2 pi k / 7 for k in 0..6 (weight cos(a) each); openness is the weighted share of rays that hit nothing (`lk_cast` returned its length). The vertex thickness is then `0.03 + (t - 0.03) * openness`. Smooth the thickness like the curvature: first the mean over vertices sharing a position, then each vertex takes the mean of its own value and its edge neighbours' mean. Store curvature at float 8 and thickness at float 9 of the vertex.

`lk_iris(tex int) float4, f32` finds the two irises in an eye texture: decode with `gltf::need_pixels(img)` and read `gltf::image_pixels(img)` (img the texture's image; when the image is negative or the pixel slice is shorter than width × height × 4 return `float4 { 0.7, 0.3, 0.3, 0.7 }, 0.14`). A pixel is iris when its alpha is above 128 and its luminance `(0.3 r + 0.59 g + 0.11 b) / 255` is below 0.42. Split iris pixels into group 1 when `x / width > y / height`, else group 2; each group's center is the mean of `(x + 0.5) / width, (y + 0.5) / height` and its radius `sqrt(count / pi) / width`. When a group is empty use the other's center; return the two centers and the mean radius of the non-empty groups, times 1.04.

`lk_pick(name const byte[:]) int` maps a material name to a detail kind: "skin" and "face" 0; "tank" 1 (jersey); "shorts" 7 (fine knit); "tank_hem", "socks", "stripes", "hood_hem", "collar", "cuff" 2; "skirt" 3; "outfit", "jeans", "pants", "strings", "seam" 4; "hoodie", "hood", "pocket" 5; "shorts_lace", "lace" 6; anything else -1. Write it as one chain of ternary `if` expressions.

`look_setup(asset int)` sets up the asset's materials. First, when `lk_tex` is empty, make the tiles in kind order 0 to 7: kind 0 with n 512 and bump 2.2, kinds 1 to 7 with n 128 and bumps 2.4, 2.4, 2.0, 2.0, 1.1, 2.0, 2.4. Then `lk_skin_geo(asset)` and `gltf::dome_normals(asset, "eyes")` (ignore its count; it turns a dished iris back into a dome so lamps leave a catchlight on the lamp's side). Then for every material index `m` from `gltf::assets[asset].materials.first` (count `.materials.count`), with `name = gltf::text_of(gltf::materials[m].name)` and `k = lk_pick(name)`, work on a copy `var x = gltf::materials[m]` and store it back at the end:
when k == 0 (skin): `shading = 1` and, when `x.shade_c.x` is 0 (the loader gives materials with glTF extras amni "skin" the library's skin preset, so keep it), `shade_a = gltf::shade_preset(1, 0)`, `shade_b = gltf::shade_preset(1, 1)`, `shade_c = gltf::shade_preset(1, 2)`; then `detail = gltf::TexRef { lk_tex[0], 0, m: lk_scale(asset, m, 0.2), .. }` (0.2 m per detail unit puts the shader's skin furrow network at about 1.1 mm and 0.29 mm cells, the real skin scale), and when the name is "skin" also `roughness = 0.45`, when it is "face" `roughness = 0.4`; then for every skin material `shade_c.y = 1.1` (furrow relief), `shade_b.x = 0.0` and `shade_b.y = 0.0` (the pit tile's own relief and cavity off, since at this scale its pits would be centimeter blobs) and `shade_a.x`, `shade_a.y` and `shade_a.z` each multiplied by 0.7 (a narrower scatter, which reads less waxy);
when the name is "teeth": `shading = 1`, `shade_a = float4 { 0.0007, 0.0005, 0.0004, 0.0 }`, `shade_b = float4 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.3, 0.5 }`, `shade_c = float4 { 0.04, 0.0, 0.0, 0.3 }`;
when the name is "tongue" or "mouth": `shading = 1`, `shade_a = float4 { 0.0015, 0.0007, 0.0003, 0.0 }`, `shade_b = float4 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.3, 0.5 }`, `shade_c = float4 { 0.028, 0.0, 0.0, 0.3 }`;
when the name is "eyes": with `c, r = lk_iris(x.base_tex.texture)` and `e = gltf::shade_preset(2, 2)`: `shading = 2`, `shade_a = c` (the measured iris centers), `shade_b = gltf::shade_preset(2, 1)`, `shade_c = float4 { r, e.y, e.z, e.w }` and, only when the material has no extension block (`if !x.ext { x.roughness = 1.0; }`: a matte base lobe, the sharp cornea lobe gives the shine; an eye material that brings its own extension values keeps its roughness);
when k >= 1 (cloth): `shading = 3`, `normal_tex = gltf::TexRef { -1, 0 }`, `detail = gltf::TexRef { lk_tex[k], 0, m: lk_scale(asset, m, t), .. }` with the tile size t per kind 1: 0.0125, 2: 0.022, 3: 0.009, 4: 0.0075, 5: 0.03, 6: 0.017, 7: 0.009; `shade_b = float4 { s, 0.85, 0.0, 0.0 }` with the detail strength s per kind 1: 0.9, 2: 1.0, 3: 0.8, 4: 0.9, 5: 0.5, 6: 0.8, 7: 0.8; `shade_a = float4 { w, f, sp, 0.0 }` (diffuse wrap, fuzz, specular weight) per kind 1: (0.25, 0.5, 0.55), 2: (0.25, 0.5, 0.55), 3: (0.2, 0.35, 0.5), 4: (0.15, 0.25, 0.7), 5: (0.35, 0.55, 0.35), 6: (0.2, 0.3, 0.8), 7: (0.2, 0.4, 0.7); when the material has no sheen (all of `sheen` below 0.001) set `sheen = float3 { 0.08, 0.08, 0.08 }` times `x.base` rgb plus 0.03 and `sheen_rough = 0.7`; for kind 5 set `sheen_rough = 0.75`.
